    {
      "name": "a_field_absent_on_one_side_and_explicitly_null_on_the_other_is_not_a_change",
      "comment": "Snapshot schemas grow: a manifest rebuilt after a new column exists carries the column explicitly null on every row that has no value, while the older snapshot simply lacks the key. That is an absent-vs-null difference in the serialization, not an upstream edit, and neither side may report it. Python compares with dict.get(), so absent and null were already equal there; the TypeScript union-of-keys loop compared `undefined !== null` and reported a change, which fabricated one entry per row per newly-added column across every historical pair.",
      "prev": [
